  },
  {
   "id": "u2",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "F I G U R E 2 Nonmetric multidimensional scaling (NMDS) plots based on chord-normalized expected species shared (CNESS) dissimilarity for $m = 1 ( \\mathsf { A } ,$ stress = 0.10) and $m = 2 0 ( \\mathsf { B } ,$ stress = 0.18) for wild bee communities in different study sites"
  },
  {
   "id": "u3",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "![Image block](doc:339aa0b/tier:advanced/page:5/block:10)"
  },
  {
   "id": "u4",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "F I G U R E 3 Relationship between the proportion of semi-natural habitat and community average body size based on abundance (A) and richness (B) respectively. Line colour indicates regressions for consolidated fields (red) and traditional fields (blue). Solid and dashed regression lines indicate significant $( p 0 . 0 5 )$ relationships"
  },
  {
   "id": "u5",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "All the statistical analyses were conducted in R Version 3.5.2 (R Core Team, 2016). We used the ‘vegan’ package (Oksanen et al., 2019) to calculate stress for nonmetric multidimensional scaling (NMDS). The function ‘ESS()’ developed by Zou and Axmacher (2020) was used for calculating CNESS values. We"
  },
  {
   "id": "u6",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "The pan trap sampling in 2019 resulted in the collection of a total of 2211 wild insect pollinators representing 49 pollinator species. Wild bee individuals accounted for 97% (2135 individuals) of the wild insect pollinator specimens and for 76% (34 species) of the total species pool (Appendix 2). NMDS based on CNESS dissimilarity did not show distinct pollinator clusters differentiating between the fields located in consolidated and traditional farmland, neither for small $( m = 1 )$ nor large sample size $( m = 2 0 )$ (Figure 2), with similar results for wild bee communities (Figure 2) and the overall wild insect pollinator communities (Appendix 5). However, with regards especially to dominant species (Figure 2a), species composition showed a greater heterogeneity (i.e., higher mean dissimilarity values) in fields located within the consolidated landscape than in the traditional one."
  },
  {
   "id": "u7",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "observed between communities sampled on traditional and consolidated farmland (Figure 3). Nonetheless, the average abundance-based body size of pollinators in the community decreased significantly $( p = 0 . 0 1 8 )$ with a decreasing proportion of semi-natural habitat at consolidated farmland, while no significant relationship was found between the proportion of semi-natural habitat and average body size in the traditional farmland matrix $( p = 0 . 1 4 )$ (Figure 3a). For the species pool, when weighing each species equally, no significant responses to the proportion of semi-natural habitat were observed for bee size in traditional $( p = 0 . 1 1 )$ or consolidated landscapes $( p = 0 . 3 8 )$ (Figure 3b)."
  },
  {
   "id": "u8",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "In terms of the two dominant species, the body size for Lasioglossum proximatum was marginally higher in consolidated than traditional farmlands $\\left( p = 0 . 0 6 \\right.$ Figure 4a), but there was no difference for Eucera floralia $( p = 0 . 7 9 ,$ Figure 4b). The proportional abundance of above-ground nesting bees based on species richness was significantly lower in consolidated farmland than in the traditional farmland $(p = 0.04)$ (Figure 5a), while there were no significant differences based on species richness $( p = 0 . 4 7 )$ (Figure 5b)."
  },
  {
   "id": "u9",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "![Image block](doc:339aa0b/tier:advanced/page:6/block:12)"
  },
  {
   "id": "u10",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "![Image block](doc:339aa0b/tier:advanced/page:6/block:14)"
  },
  {
   "id": "u11",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "F I G U R E 5 The proportion of above-ground nesting wild bees based on abundance (A) and richness (B) in consolidated and traditional farmland"
  },
  {
   "id": "u12",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "1 Appendix 1. Examples of fields in traditional (A) and consolidated farmland (B)."
  },
  {
   "id": "u13",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "![Image block](doc:b84f361/tier:advanced/page:1/block:2)"
  },
  {
   "id": "u14",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "Appendix 3. Redundancy analysis (RDA) of wild bee communities, constrained by the amount of semi-natural habitat (SNH) and field size (FS). RDA axis 1 and 2 explained 15.5% and 4.8% of the distribution, respectively. The adjusted $\\mathtt { R } ^ { 2 }$ was 0.08. The full model is significant $F = 1.73, p = 0.021$ , F distribution based on 999 permutations). Variation in pollinator community composition was captured by the first axis (F= 2.65, P=0.02), while responses on the second axis were non-significant $( \\mathsf{F} = 0.8, \\mathsf{P} = 0.695 )$ . Based on permutation test (n=999), the impact of SNH was significant $(p = 0.01)$ , while the effect of FS was not $(p = 0.451)$"
  },
  {
   "id": "u15",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "Appendix 4. The impact of farmland consolidation and the proportion of semi-natural habitat on the body size of Lasioglossum proximatum and Eucera floralia. Both full and final models include consolidation status (CS; base: traditional farmland), proportion of semi-natural habitat (SNH) and the interaction between semi-natural habitat and consolidation status $( \\mathsf { S N H ^ { * } C S } )$ . None of the result was significant (p"
  },
  {
   "id": "u16",
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "Appendix 5. Non-metric multidimensional scaling (NMDS) plots based on Chord-Normalized Expected Species Shared (CNESS) dissimilarity for m=1 (A, stress=0.14) and m=20 (B, stress=0.21) for wild pollinator communities at the 18 research sites."
  },
